{{smaller block|Sometimes he ''trots'', as if he told the steps,
With gentle majesty and modest pride.
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 277.}}}}

{{smaller block|This is true, whether they [animals] move per latera, that is, two legs of one side together, which is tolutation or ambling, or per diametrum, lifting one foot before and the cross foot behind, which is succussation or ''trotting''.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., iv. 6.}}}}

{{smaller block|I saw Lady Suffolk ''trot'' a mile in 2.26. Flora Temple has ''trotted'' close down to 2.20, and Ethan Allen in 2.25, or less.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Professor, vii.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To cause to trot; ride at a trot.

{{smaller block|He that can ''trot'' a courser, break a rush,
And, arm'd in proof, dare dure a straw's strong push.
{{right|''Marston'', Satires, i. 28.}}}}

'''2.''' To ride over or about at a trot.

{{smaller block|This lovely boy . . . bestrid a Scythian steed,
''Trotting'' the ring, and tilting at a glove.
{{right|''Marlowe'', Tamburlaine, II., i. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|He made him turn, and stop, and bound,
To gallop and to ''trot'' the round;
He scarce could stand on any ground,
He was so full of mettle.
{{right|''Drayton'', Nymphidia.}}}}

'''3.''' To use a "pony" or some similar means in studying; "pony": as, to ''trot'' a lesson. [College slang, U. S.]—'''To trot out''', {{smaller|to cause to trot, as}}

readings.c2s1:
a horse, to show his paces; hence, to bring or draw out for exhibition. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|They would sit for hours solemnly ''trotting out'' for one another's admiration their commonplaces of the philosophical copy-book, until I tingled from head to foot.
{{right|''D. Christie Murray'', Weaker Vessel, xiii.}}}}

'''trot'''<sup>1</sup> (trot), ''n.'' [< ME. ''trot'', < OF. ''trot'' = Pr. ''trot'' = Sp. Pg. ''trote'' = It. ''trotto'' (G. ''trott''); from the verb.] 1. Quick, steady movement; "go": as, to keep one on the ''trot'' all day. [Now colloq.]—2. A gait faster than the walk and slower than the run. {{smaller|In the trot of bipeds both feet are alternately off the ground at the same time for an interval in each step; in that of quadrupeds, in a very slow trot there is always one foot on the ground, a part of the time two feet, and a part of the time three. If fast, there are two intervals in each stride when all the feet are off the ground (the stride being the distance in time or space between the successive points on the ground touched by the same foot), the horse leaving the ground from the hind feet in succession, while in the run he leaves the ground from a fore foot. In the trot the limbs move in pairs, diagonally but not quite simultaneously, even in the "square trot." If the difference becomes considerable, it constitutes "single-footing"; if the difference becomes so great that the action is reversed, and the pair of limbs on the same side move together, it becomes "pacing." While the trot is naturally a slower gait than the run, it has become the instinctive fast gait in certain breeds of horses. See ''trotter'', and cut in preceding column.}}

{{smaller block|The canter is to the gallop very much what the walk is to the ''trot.''
{{right|''Youatt'', The Horse (Treatise on Draught).}}}}

{{smaller block|In those days, the Star Cambridge Coach, which left the Belle Sauvage Yard in Ludgate Hill about 4 {{sc|p. m.}}, threaded all the streets between its starting-point and Shoreditch Church at a ''trot.''
{{right|''Quarterly Rev.'', CXLVI. 198.}}}}

3. A toddling child; in general, a child: a term of endearment.

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|Ethel romped with the little children — the rosy little ''trots''.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Newcomes, x.}}}}
'''4.''' A "pony"; a "crib." [College slang, U. S.] — '''5.''' A trot-line. [U. S.] — '''6.''' A small line that sets off from the main trot-line, to the extreme end of which the hook is fastened. See ''trot-line.'' [U. S.] — {{smaller|'''Eggwife-trot.''' Same as ''egg-trot.''}}

'''trot<sup>2</sup>†''' (trot), ''n.'' [A var. of ''trat.''] An old woman: a term of disparagement.

{{smaller block|An aged ''trot'' and tough did marie with a lad.
{{right|''Turberville'', Of a Contrerie Mariage.}}
An old ''trot'' with ne'er a tooth in her head.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., i. 2. 80.}}}}

'''trotcozy, trotcosy''' (trot′kō-zi), ''n.''; pl. ''trotcozies, trotcosies'' (-ziz). [Appar. so called as enabling one to 'trot,' drive, or travel 'cozy' or warm, < ''trot'' + ''cozy''; less prob. orig. *''throat-cozy'', < ''throat'' + ''cozy''.] A warm covering for the head, neck, and breast in cold weather when one is traveling. [Scotch.]

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|The upper part of his form . . . was shrouded in a large great-coat belted over his under habiliments, and crested with a huge cowl of the same stuffs, which, when drawn over the head and hat, completely overshadowed both, and, being buttoned beneath the chin, was called a ''trot-cozy''.
{{right|''Scott'', Waverley, i. 318.}}}}

'''trotevale†''', ''n.'' [ME., appar. < OF. *''trotevale'' (perhaps referring orig. to Scandinavian myths), < Icel. ''Thrūdhvaldr'', a title of Thor (''Thrūdhvaldr godha'', the heroic defender of the gods), < ''Thrūdhr'', used only as the name of a goddess and of a woman, also in compound names (= AS. ''Thrȳtho'', the name of a woman; cf. OHG. ''trūta'', G. dial. ''trute'', ''drude'', a witch), + ''-valdr'', < ''valda'', rule: see ''wield''. Cf. ''walterot''.] A trifling thing.

{{smaller block|Yn gamys and festys and at the ale
Love men to lestene ''trotevale''.
{{right|''MS. Harl.'' 1701, f. 1. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|ȝwan thre traitours at o tale to-gidere weren agein me sworn,
Al ye maden ''trotenale'' [read ''troteuale''] that I haved seid biforn;
ȝe ledde me bi doune and dale, as an oxe bi the horn,
Til ther as him is browen bale, ther his throte schal be schorn.
{{right|''Walter Mapes'', Poems (ed. Wright), p. 337.}}}}

